WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:02.646
[soft music]

00:00:04.990 --> 00:00:08.516
[light scraping]

00:00:08.516 --> 00:00:11.183
[water pouring]

00:00:15.260 --> 00:00:17.000
My name is Sophia Bogle.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:19.240
I've been restoring
books for over 25 years,

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:21.790
and I started my company, Save Your Books,

00:00:21.790 --> 00:00:24.690
so that anyone can learn
archival-book repair.

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:27.220
The book I'm restoring today is

00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:30.010
the Hans Christian
Andersen "Fairy Tales" book

00:00:30.010 --> 00:00:34.232
from the late 1800s, early 1900s.

00:00:34.232 --> 00:00:36.732
[soft music]

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:39.240
The first thing that we do

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:41.890
in pretty much any full restoration

00:00:41.890 --> 00:00:44.081
is we have to separate the cover

00:00:44.081 --> 00:00:45.810
from the text block.

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:48.401
The tool that I prefer to use

00:00:48.401 --> 00:00:50.301
is the book-repair knife.

00:00:50.301 --> 00:00:52.550
It has a rounded tip

00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:56.730
and is less likely to do
damage that you did not intend

00:00:56.730 --> 00:00:58.751
as an X-Acto knife would.

00:00:59.610 --> 00:01:01.430
I'm removing the old tape.

00:01:01.430 --> 00:01:02.490
It's very acidic,

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:04.300
and if I leave it on the book at all,

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:06.453
it's going to affect the book later on.

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:11.870
When you're removing some
of the original material

00:01:11.870 --> 00:01:12.785
and you know you're going to be

00:01:12.785 --> 00:01:13.884
replacing it with something,

00:01:13.884 --> 00:01:16.205
it's good to mark where it was

00:01:16.205 --> 00:01:19.630
in relationship to the other materials.

00:01:19.630 --> 00:01:20.513
And so in this case,

00:01:20.513 --> 00:01:23.571
I've used a pencil and a piece of paper

00:01:23.571 --> 00:01:26.793
to mark where the original went.

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:32.170
I'm using a 100-grit sanding block

00:01:32.170 --> 00:01:35.381
to clean the edges of these boards off.

00:01:35.381 --> 00:01:37.299
It makes a nice, clean edge.

00:01:37.299 --> 00:01:39.716
[soft music]

00:01:45.527 --> 00:01:49.060
[light scraping]

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:53.050
A lot of general cleaning can be done

00:01:53.050 --> 00:01:55.020
using a book-repair knife,

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:56.700
and using the book-repair knife

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:59.040
to just remove some clumpy bits

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:01.755
that are stuck to the end paper.

00:02:01.755 --> 00:02:03.000
[water pouring]

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:04.310
When you're repairing a book,

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:06.590
usually all the pages are still connected,

00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:09.520
and there are some pages that will benefit

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:12.310
from this washing technique that I have.

00:02:12.310 --> 00:02:15.590
It'll help us to get the
tape off of the page,

00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:16.900
in a way that you couldn't do

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:18.990
if it was attached to the book still.

00:02:18.990 --> 00:02:20.731
To wash a page,

00:02:20.731 --> 00:02:24.240
to get rid of the tape,

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:27.593
you just have to put
it in fairly hot water.

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:32.910
You aren't going to need any
additives to clean these pages.

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:34.600
It's just hot water.

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:36.473
It's surprisingly effective.

00:02:37.350 --> 00:02:40.523
It can sit in the water for hours.

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:43.353
You're also just going to have to be

00:02:43.353 --> 00:02:45.670
very delicate with the paper,

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:47.480
because it's in a more delicate state

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:49.023
when it's in the water.

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:54.040
I'm using a microspatula
to help separate the pages,

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:56.281
and I'm also using a ruler

00:02:56.281 --> 00:02:59.006
to help hold down the other page.

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:03.110
You can use a soft brush

00:03:03.110 --> 00:03:06.303
to help you to brush
away the old adhesive.

00:03:07.572 --> 00:03:10.072
[soft music]

00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:30.120
When you're touching up
anything black on a cover,

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:33.240
you're going to want
to use an archival pen.

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:35.873
It's a 0.08-micron pen,

00:03:35.873 --> 00:03:38.565
and it is archival ink.

00:03:38.565 --> 00:03:40.960
The smaller the tip, the better, in a way.

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:42.530
Do not use a Sharpie.

00:03:42.530 --> 00:03:45.666
Those will damage and discolor.

00:03:45.666 --> 00:03:48.166
[soft music]

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:51.770
While I was cleaning,

00:03:51.770 --> 00:03:55.390
I also noticed that some
of the gilt stamping

00:03:55.390 --> 00:03:57.583
has been removed.

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:00.610
When I'm using the word "gilt,"

00:04:00.610 --> 00:04:02.360
gilt refers to gold.

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:04.760
So it's gold stamping on the cover.

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:06.833
Some people would call it embossing.

00:04:08.510 --> 00:04:12.800
Originally, this was stamped
with hot metal plates,

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:15.900
with foil coming down onto the book.

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:19.650
The best way to fill in gilt stamping

00:04:19.650 --> 00:04:21.525
is to use shell gold.

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:25.730
I'm using a liner brush,

00:04:25.730 --> 00:04:27.730
and I have all the control in the world.

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:30.210
You're going to make mistakes.

00:04:30.210 --> 00:04:33.301
You can actually remove the gilt

00:04:33.301 --> 00:04:35.584
by using a cuticle stick,

00:04:35.584 --> 00:04:39.753
and then you can clean up your gilt.

00:04:41.183 --> 00:04:43.683
[soft music]

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:51.250
If you want to match
the color of your book

00:04:51.250 --> 00:04:52.900
and do touch-up on the covers,

00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:55.560
it's not as hard as you might think.

00:04:55.560 --> 00:04:58.510
You look at this, this
is a greenish brown.

00:04:58.510 --> 00:05:03.320
The brownish colors that tend
to be on these older books,

00:05:03.320 --> 00:05:05.310
they're muddy looking.

00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:08.820
And the way to get to
that muddy-looking color

00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:11.490
is to go across the color wheel

00:05:11.490 --> 00:05:13.700
from the color you think it is

00:05:13.700 --> 00:05:17.930
and add that other color to
make it more muddy looking.

00:05:17.930 --> 00:05:20.430
[soft music]

00:05:32.550 --> 00:05:35.120
When you find tears on your pages

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:36.730
and you want to repair them,

00:05:36.730 --> 00:05:41.280
I highly recommend using
Japanese tissue that's thin

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:43.190
and an archival paste,

00:05:43.190 --> 00:05:45.660
such as what I have here,
which is nori paste.

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:47.480
It's a rice-starch paste.

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:50.100
A really thin tissue is going to be better

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:51.760
than a thicker tissue.

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:53.653
You don't need very much paste.

00:05:54.649 --> 00:05:57.877
[soft music]

00:05:59.410 --> 00:06:01.960
You get everything ready.

00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:04.850
You've got your book in the cover,

00:06:04.850 --> 00:06:06.140
right side up.

00:06:07.610 --> 00:06:10.250
You're going to be gluing
all of those materials.

00:06:10.250 --> 00:06:14.150
You're going to glue that
mull and that little hinge,

00:06:14.150 --> 00:06:16.760
and you're gluing that
decorative end sheet back,

00:06:16.760 --> 00:06:19.713
and then you're going to close the cover.

00:06:21.020 --> 00:06:23.520
[soft music]

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:27.833
And then you slip a sheet of wax paper in.

00:06:29.980 --> 00:06:32.216
And then you put the pressing board in.

00:06:37.150 --> 00:06:38.370
When I discovered that there were

00:06:38.370 --> 00:06:40.330
pages missing from this book,

00:06:40.330 --> 00:06:44.470
I found a version of
the story that connected

00:06:44.470 --> 00:06:49.020
to where the book started
with the pages missing.

00:06:49.950 --> 00:06:53.210
I used paper that's
called antique endleaf.

00:06:54.270 --> 00:06:55.740
[soft music]

00:06:55.740 --> 00:06:59.630
I am using a colored
pencil to color the edges

00:06:59.630 --> 00:07:03.380
of the new pages that I added to the book.

00:07:03.380 --> 00:07:05.110
I don't want them to pop out.

00:07:05.110 --> 00:07:06.700
I don't want it to look obvious

00:07:06.700 --> 00:07:08.622
that I added these new pages.

00:07:08.622 --> 00:07:11.122
[soft music]

00:07:13.750 --> 00:07:15.160
And that's it.

00:07:15.160 --> 00:07:16.724
That's our book.

00:07:16.724 --> 00:07:19.141
[soft music]

